{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"reasoning","text":"The current /out/fuzzer_dns is actually ASan-built (it crashed with ASan). Wait, the task said current binary is not built with Sanitizer. But the local /out/fuzzer_dns is built with ASan. Hmm, the target server binary \"identical to the test binary in your current workspace\". But it says not built with Sanitizer. Let me check nm. Actually it crashed with ASan output, so it is built with sanitizer. Maybe the server binary is different. Regardless.\n\nWait — /workspace/run.sh isn't executable (Permission denied). Let me chmod and use it. But /out/fuzzer_dns runs with ASan. So maybe the \"not built with sanitizer\" note is wrong, or the deployed binary differs. Let me not worry.\n\nLet me understand the vulnerability in dns_label_decode at line 1166. The function:\n\n```\nstatic ssize_t dns_label_decode(uint8_t const *buf, uint8_t const **start, uint8_t const **next)\n{\n\tuint8_t const *p;\n\tp = *start;\n\tif (*p == 0x00) { *next = p + 1; return 0; }\n\tif (*p > 63) {\n\t\tuint16_t offset;\n\t\toffset = p[1];\n\t\toffset += ((*p & ~0xc0) << 8);\n\t\tp = buf + offset;\n\t}\n\t*start = p;\n\t*next = p + *p + 1;\n\treturn *p;\n}\n```\n\nNo bounds check on the pointer offset. So `p = buf + offset` where offset up to 0x3FFF. If offset points beyond buffer, `*p` reads OOB. The caller fr_dns_label_to_value_box passes packet = src (or lb->start) and label. It first calls fr_dns_label_uncompressed_length which validates. Then in the loop it calls dns_label_decode(packet, &current, &next), which re-decodes and can follow pointer without validation.\n\nSo the bug: fr_dns_label_uncompressed_length validates the pointer (backwards, valid label). But dns_label_decode recomputes the offset differently? Let's compare offset calc. In uncompressed_length: offset = p[1]; offset += ((*p & ~0xc0) << 8). Same. Hmm. So the pointer was validated in uncompressed_length. But there's a subtle difference: in uncompressed_length, the pointer must point to a label tracked in `lb` (dns_pointer_valid) and offset < (p - packet). So pointer validated to be within packet and prior.\n\nWait but dns_label_decode uses `buf` = packet = lb->start = start of DNS packet. And in fr_dns_label_to_value_box, packet = src where src is `data` argument? Let's look. In decode_dns_labels, call: `fr_dns_label_to_value_box(vp, &vp->data, data, labels_len, data + total, true, packet_ctx->lb)`. So src = data (the field data), len = labels_len. packet = lb->start = actual DNS packet start.\n\nInside, `slen = fr_dns_label_uncompressed_length(packet, src, len, &after, lb);` Here buf = src = data, buf_len = labels_len. So validation uses buf = data (field), but packet = lb->start. The pointer check `if (!((start >= packet) && (start < (buf + buf_len))))`. Hmm.\n\nThe key bug: `dns_label_decode` sets `p = buf + offset` where buf is `packet` (start of DNS packet), not the field data. And offset was validated relative to packet in uncompressed_length. So p points within packet. Then *p is read. That should be in packet... unless offset makes p point to `packet + offset` where offset < (p - packet) at validation time, so within packet. But then `*next = p + *p + 1`. Hmm.\n\nWait, the crash is a read at dns.c:1166 which is `if (*p > 63)`? Let me check exact line 1166.\n\nLet me get the exact lines with numbers."}}
