ere the target is a fuzzer binary. Maybe the intended exploitation is that `fuzzer_dns` when given input reads a file, and we can... Actually wait. Let me reconsider. Maybe there's a different path: The DNS decode has a buffer overflow write somewhere. Let me look at `fr_dns_label_uncompressed_length` more: it reads `*q` where q = packet+offset which can be beyond buffer. It validates offset < p-packet (backwards). So within buffer.\n\nActually the vulnerability is specifically: `dns_label_decode` doesn't check that `p[1]` (second byte of pointer) is within the buffer, and doesn't validate offset against buffer length. In `fr_dns_label_uncompressed_length`, pointers are validated. But `dns_label_decode` in `fr_dns_label_to_value_box` is called after `fr_dns_label_uncompressed_length` returned success. Hmm, how can a pointer get through validation but fail here?\n\nLet's trace the poc. Let me actually instrument. Let me set up the PoC analysis with gdb or a debug build. Actually, let me first understand the fuzzer input format for DNS.\n\nLet's look at decode.c fr_dns_decode_proto around 560-620."}}
